It took me way too long to find this, but here's a beautifully short way:

Consider block matrices with two rows and two columns of  $n\times n$  blocks.
Let  $t$  be an arbitrary real number. We have
\begin{align*}\begin{bmatrix}t I - (A-MX)&M0&I\end{bmatrix}\cdot \begin{bmatrix}I&0-X&I\end{bmatrix}\cdot\begin{bmatrix}I&-M0&t I-B\end{bmatrix}&= \begin{bmatrix}t I-A&M-X&I\end{bmatrix}\cdot\begin{bmatrix}I&-M0&t I-B\end{bmatrix}
&= \begin{bmatrix}t I-A&-(tM-AM)+(tM-MB)-X&XM+(t I-B)\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}t I - (A-MX)&M0&I\end{bmatrix}\cdot \begin{bmatrix}I&0-X&I\end{bmatrix}\cdot\begin{bmatrix}I&-M0&t I-B\end{bmatrix}&=\begin{bmatrix}t I-A&0-X&t I -(B-XM)\end{bmatrix}\end{align*}
So far, we've used that  $AM-MB=0$ , and nothing else about these matrices. Now, equate determinants in that last equality. The determinant of a block triangular matrix (assuming that all blocks on the diagonal are square) is the product of the determinants of the blocks, so we get $\det(t I-(A-MX))\cdot \det(tI-B)=\det(tI-A)\cdot \det(tI-(B-XM))$  But  $\det(tI-A)$  and  $\det(tI-B)$  are the characteristic polynomials of  $A$  and  $B$  respectively evaluated at  $t$ ; by the problem statement, they're equal for all  $t$ . At all but finitely many  $t$ , these determinants are nonzero, so we can divide by them, and thus  $\det(t I-(A-MX))=\det(tI-(B-XM))$ . These are polynomials in  $t$ , so equality at infinitely many  $t$  implies that they are equal for all  $t$ , and  $A-MX$  and  $B-XM$  have the same characteristic polynomial. In particular, evaluating at  $t=0$ , they have the same determinant. Done.

The only property of  $\mathbb{R}$  we used was its infinite size, so the whole thing works over any infinite field. Over a finite field - that field has an infinite extension. Run the argument with  $t$  in that extension, then project back down.

By the Ravi substitution,

\begin{align*}
	\sum_{(a,b,c) \in T} \frac{2^a}{3^b5^c}
	&= \sum_{x,y,z \ge 1 \text{ odd}}
	\frac{2^\frac{y+z}{2}}{3^{\frac{z+x}{2}} 5^{\frac{x+y}{2}}}
	+ \sum_{x,y,z \ge 2 \text{ even}}
	\frac{2^\frac{y+z}{2}}{3^{\frac{z+x}{2}} 5^{\frac{x+y}{2}}} 
	&= \sum_{u,v,w \ge 1} \frac{2^{v+w}}{3^{w+u}5^{u+v}}
	\left( 1 + \frac{2^{-1}}{3^{-1}5^{-1}} \right) 
	&= \frac{17}{2} \sum_{u,v,w \ge 1}
	\left(\frac{1}{15}\right)^u \left(\frac25\right)^v \left(\frac23\right)^w 
	&= \frac{17}{2} \frac{\frac{1}{15}}{1-\frac{1}{15}}
	\frac{\frac25}{1-\frac25}\frac{\frac23}{1-\frac23} 
	&= \frac{17}{21}.
\end{align*}

No idea why this was B4; it was by far the easiest problem on the test for me. I knew how to do it in less than a second on seeing it and the rest was just arithmetic. (We use the Ravi substitution all the time on olympiad inequalities, so it's practically a reflex for me.)

I did Ravi solution for the my writup, but as a question,  how carefully would one have to deal with convergence issues here? I think everything is fine with the Ravi approach because our three numbers are all then less than  $1$ .

Here's another approach: let  $x=2,y=\frac{1}{3},z=\frac{1}{5}$ , and consider the formal power series
\[
f(x,y,z)=\sum_{(a,b,c)\in T} x^ay^bz^c
\]
(so we're basically throwing convergence issues out the window.) Let  $S_a$  be the set of positive triples with  $a\ge b+c$ , and define  $S_b,S_c$  similarly. Then
\begin{align*}
f&=\sum_{a,b,c\ge 1} x^ay^bz^c-\sum_{cyc}\sum_{(a,b,c)\in S_a} x^ay^bz^c
&=\frac{xyz}{(1-x)(1-y)(1-z)}-\sum_{cyc}\sum_{b,c\ge 1} \frac{x(xy)(xz)}{(1-x)(1-xy)(1-xz)}
&=\frac{xyz+x^2y^2z^2}{(1-xy)(1-yz)(1-zx)}
\end{align*}
where the last equality follows after sufficient expansion. Plugging in  $x,y,z$  gives us the answer.

Taking casework on whether  $b$  or  $c$  is larger and using the Triangle Inequality, we wish to find   $$ \sum_{b \ge 1} \sum_{c \ge b+1} \sum_{a=c-b+1}^{b+c-1} \frac{2^a}{3^b5^c}+\sum_{c \ge 1}\sum_{b \ge c} \sum_{a = b-c+1}^{b+c-1} \frac{2^a}{3^b5^c}. $$   For the first sum, we have
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{b \ge 1} \sum_{c \ge b+1} \sum_{a=c-b+1}^{b+c-1} \frac{2^a}{3^b5^c} &= \sum_{b \ge 1} \sum_{c \ge b+1} \frac{2^{c-b+1}+\ldots+2^{b+c-1}}{3^b5^c} 
    &= \sum_{b \ge 1} \sum_{c \ge b+1} \frac{2^{c-b}(2^{2b}-2)}{3^b5^c} 
    &= \sum_{b \ge 1} \left(\left(\frac{1}{6}\right)^b(4^b-2)\sum_{c \ge b+1} \left(\frac{2}{5}\right)^c\right) 
    &= \sum_{b \ge 1} \left(\left(\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^b-2\left(\frac{1}{6}\right)^b\right)\cdot \frac{(\tfrac{2}{5})^{b+1}}{1-\tfrac{2}{5}}\right) 
    &= \frac{5}{3} \cdot \frac{2}{5} \cdot \sum_{b \ge 1} \left(\left(\frac{4}{15}\right)^b-2\left(\frac{1}{15}\right)^b\right) 
    &= \frac{2}{3} \cdot \left(\frac{\tfrac{4}{15}}{1-\tfrac{4}{15}}-2\cdot\frac{\tfrac{1}{15}}{1-\tfrac{1}{15}}\right) 
    &= \frac{2}{3} \cdot \left(\frac{4}{11}-\frac{1}{7}\right) 
    &= \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{17}{77}.
\end{align*}
For the second sum, we have
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{c \ge 1}\sum_{b \ge c} \sum_{a=b-c+1}^{b+c-1} \frac{2^a}{3^b5^c} &= \sum_{c \ge 1}\sum_{b \ge c} \frac{2^{b-c+1}+\ldots+2^{b+c-1}}{3^b5^c} 
    &= \sum_{c \ge 1} \sum_{b \ge c} \frac{2^{b-c}(2^{2c}-2)}{3^b5^c} 
    &= \sum_{c \ge 1}\left(\left(\frac{1}{10}\right)^c (4^c-2)\sum_{b \ge c} \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^b\right) 
    &= \sum_{c \ge 1} \left(\left(\left(\frac{2}{5}\right)^c-2\left(\frac{1}{10}\right)^c\right)\cdot\frac{(\tfrac{2}{3})^c}{1-\tfrac{2}{3}}\right) 
    &= 3 \cdot \sum_{c \ge 1} \left(\left(\frac{4}{15}\right)^c-2\left(\frac{1}{15}\right)^c\right) 
    &= 3 \cdot \left(\frac{\tfrac{4}{15}}{1-\tfrac{4}{15}}-2\cdot\frac{\tfrac{1}{15}}{1-\tfrac{1}{15}}\right) 
    &= 3 \cdot \left(\frac{4}{11}-\frac{1}{7}\right) 
    &= 3 \cdot \frac{17}{77}.
\end{align*}
Hence, the answer is
\begin{align*}
    \frac{2}{3}\cdot\frac{17}{77} + 3 \cdot \frac{17}{77} &= \frac{11}{3} \cdot \frac{17}{77} 
    &= \boxed{\frac{17}{21}}.
\end{align*} $\square$

Note that  $T=\{(u + v, v + w, w + u)\mid u, v, w\text{ are all odd or all even}\}.$  Thus, we can write our sum as:
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{(a, b, c)\in T} \frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c} & = \sum_{u, v, w \geq 1\text{ even}} \frac{2^{\frac{u + v}{2}}}{3^{\frac{v + w}{2}}5^{\frac{w + u}{2}}}+\sum_{u, v, w \geq1 \text{ odd}} \frac{2^{\frac{u + v}{2}}}{3^{\frac{v + w}{2}}5^{\frac{w + u}{2}}} 
    & = \sum_{x, y, z \geq 1} \left(1 + \frac{2^{-1}}{3^{-1}5^{-1}}\right)\frac{2^{x + y}}{3^{y + z}5^{z + x}} 
    & = \frac{17}2\sum_{x\geq1}\left(\frac{2}{5}\right)^x\sum_{y\geq1}\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^y\sum_{z\geq1}\left(\frac{1}{15}\right)^z 
    & = \frac{17}2 \frac23\cdot2\cdot\frac1{14} 
    & = \frac{17}{21}
\end{align*}

Let  $a=\frac{r+s}{2}, b=\frac{s+t}{2},c=\frac{t+r}{2},$  where  $r,s,t$  must be positive integers of the same parity. We have
\begin{align*}
\sum_{(a,b,c)\in T} \frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c} &= \sum_{r,s,t \ \text{odd}} \frac{2^{\frac{r+s}{2}}}{3^{\frac{s+t}{2}} 5^{\frac{t+r}{2}}} + \sum_{r,s,t \ \text{even}} \frac{2^{\frac{r+s}{2}}}{3^{\frac{s+t}{2}} 5^{\frac{t+r}{2}}}  
&= \sum_{r,s,t \ \text{odd}} \left( \frac{2}{5}\right)^{\frac{r}{2}} \left( \frac{2}{3}\right)^{\frac{s}{2}} \left( \frac{1}{15}\right)^{\frac{t}{2}} + \sum_{r,s,t \ \text{even}} \left( \frac{2}{5}\right)^{\frac{r}{2}} \left( \frac{2}{3}\right)^{\frac{s}{2}} \left( \frac{1}{15}\right)^{\frac{t}{2}} 
&= \frac{\sqrt{\frac{2}{5}}}{1-\frac{2}{5}} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}}{1-\frac{2}{3}} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{\frac{1}{15}}}{1-\frac{1}{15}} + \frac{\frac{2}{5}}{1-\frac{2}{5}} \cdot \frac{\frac{2}{3}}{1-\frac{2}{3}}  \cdot \frac{\frac{1}{15}}{1-\frac{1}{15}} 
&=\frac{5}{7}+\frac{2}{21} 
&=\boxed{\frac{17}{21}}. \ \blacksquare
\end{align*}

The Ravi Substitution tells us we can substitute  $a=\tfrac{x+y}{2}$ ,  $b = \tfrac{x+z}{2}$ , and  $c = \tfrac{y+z}{2}$  to get rid of the condition. Hence:
\begin{align*}
\sum_{(a,b,c) \in T} \frac{2^a}{3^b5^c} &= \sum_{x, y, z \text{ odd} \ge 1} \frac{2^{\tfrac{x+y}{2}}}{3^{\tfrac{x+z}{2}}5^{\tfrac{y+z}{2}}} + \sum_{x, y, z \text{ even} \ge 2} \frac{2^{\tfrac{x+y}{2}}}{3^{\tfrac{x+z}{2}}5^{\tfrac{y+z}{2}}} 
&= \sum_{j,k,l \ge 1} \frac{2^{j+k-1}}{3^{j+l-1}5^{k+l-1}} + \sum_{j, k, l \ge 1} \frac{2^{j+k}}{3^{j+l}5^{k+l}} 
&= \dfrac{17}{2}\sum_{j, k, l \ge 1} \frac{2^{j+k}}{3^{j+l}5^{k+l}} 
&= \dfrac{17}{2}\sum_{j, k, l \ge 1} \left(\dfrac{2}{3}\right)^j\left(\dfrac{2}{5}\right)^k\left(\dfrac{1}{15}\right)^l 
&= \dfrac{17}{2}\cdot\dfrac{2}{3}\cdot\dfrac{2}{5}\cdot\dfrac{1}{15}\cdot\dfrac{1}{1-\tfrac{2}{3}}\cdot\dfrac{1}{1-\tfrac{2}{5}}\cdot\dfrac{1}{1-\tfrac{1}{15}} 
&= \dfrac{17}{2}\cdot\dfrac{2}{3}\cdot\dfrac{2}{5}\cdot\dfrac{1}{15}\cdot3\cdot\dfrac{5}{3}\cdot\dfrac{15}{14} 
&= \boxed{\dfrac{17}{21}}
\end{align*}
as desired.  $\square$

Going to skip a few details because my sol is computation heavy. We split into  $c \geq b$  and  $ c < b$ . If we have  $c \geq b$  the sum evaluates to: \begin{align*}
\sum_{b=1}^{\infty} \sum_{c=b}^{\infty} \frac{1}{3^b} \frac{1}{5^c} (2^{b+c} - 2^{c-b + 1}) = \frac{85}{231}
\end{align*}
by literally bashing these sum mathcounts style there isn't really any thinking needed. Then the other case is  $\frac{34}{77}$  by a similar computation except with finite sums, so we sum to  $\boxed{\frac{17}{21}}$

Perform the Ravi substitution:
\[(a, b, c) \mapsto \left(\frac{y + z}{2}, \frac{x + z}{2}, \frac{x + y}{2}\right)\]
where  $x$ ,  $y$ , and  $z$  are positive integers of the same parity. We wish to evaluate
\[\sum_{x, y, z \text{ odd}} \frac{2^{\frac{y + z}{2}}}{3^{\frac{x + z}{2}} 5^\frac{x + y}{2}} + \sum_{x, y, z \text{ even}} \frac{2^{\frac{y + z}{2}}}{3^{\frac{x + z}{2}} 5^\frac{x + y}{2}} = O + E.\]
Concentrate on  $E$  first. Note that
\begin{align*}
    E &= \sum_{u \ge 1} \sum_{v \ge 1} \sum_{w \ge 1} \frac{2^{v + w}} {3^{u + w} 5^{u + v}} 
    &= \left(\sum_{u \ge 1} \frac{1}{15^u} \right) \left( \sum_{v \ge 1} \frac{2^v}{5^v}\right) \left( \sum_{w \ge 1} \frac{2^w}{3^w}\right) = \frac{2}{21}.
\end{align*}
Now,
\begin{align*}
    O = \sum_{u \ge 1} \sum_{v \ge 1} \sum_{w \ge 1} \frac{2^{v + w - 1}} {3^{u + w - 1} 5^{u + v - 1}} = \frac{15}{2}E
\end{align*}
so the answer is  $\frac{17}{2} \frac{2}{21} = \frac{17}{21}$ .  $\square$

