<blockquote>Let  $ABC$  be a triangle with circumcircle  $\Omega$  and incentre  $I$ . Let the line passing through  $I$  and perpendicular to  $CI$  intersect the segment  $BC$  and the arc  $BC$  (not containing  $A$ ) of  $\Omega$  at points  $U$  and  $V$  , respectively. Let the line passing through  $U$  and parallel to  $AI$  intersect  $AV$  at  $X$ , and let the line passing through  $V$  and parallel to  $AI$  intersect  $AB$  at  $Y$  . Let  $W$  and  $Z$  be the midpoints of  $AX$  and  $BC$ , respectively. Prove that if the points  $I, X,$  and  $Y$  are collinear, then the points  $I, W ,$  and  $Z$  are also collinear.
</blockquote>
Assume that  $I,X,Y$  are collinear. We will show  $W \in IZ$  by showing  $WI \parallel AE,$  where  $E$  is the  $A$  excircle touch point on  $BC.$  
Firstly, note that  $\measuredangle BIU=\measuredangle BIC-\pi/2=A/2=\measuredangle BAI=\measuredangle BYV$  and so  $IYBV$  is cyclic. 

Define  $P=XU \cap AB.$  Then we have the following:**Claim 1:**  $X$  is the midpoint of  $PU.$ <span style="color:#00f">Proof:</span> We can see that  $$ XP:AI=YX:YI=VX:VA=XU:AI $$  and so  $XP=XU.$   $\square$ **Claim 2:**  $UX \perp YI.$ <span style="color:#00f">Proof:</span> We can see that  $\measuredangle PBI=\measuredangle YVI=\measuredangle PUI$  and so  $BPIU$  is cyclic. Since  $BI$  bisects  $\angle PBU,$  hence  $IP=IU.$  Combining this with **Claim 1** yields that  $IPU$  is isosceles with  $X$  the midpoint of  $PU,$  and so  $UP \perp IX.$   $\square$ By  $UP \parallel AI,$  **Claim 2** yields that  $AI \perp IY$  and so  $Y$  is the  $A$  mixtilinear contact point with  $AB.$  Thus  $BYIV$  cyclic gives that  $V$  is the  $A$  mixtilinear incircle contact point with the circumcircle.

Thus,  $AV, AE$  are isogonal and so  $\measuredangle XAI=\measuredangle IAE.$  Further,  $XI \perp AI$  and so the reflection of  $X$  about  $I,$  say  $X',$  must lie on  $AE.$  So in  $\triangle XAX',$  we find that  $WI$  is the  $X$  midline and so parallel to  $AE.$   $\blacksquare$ **Comment:** If  $I_A$  is the  $A$  excenter, then  $B,V,I_A$  are collinear points. Also,  $\angle VIC=\pi/2$  translates nicely to  $IV \parallel I_AC.$ [asy]
