<span style="color:#f00">**Claim.**</span> We have  $XJ=XU.$  
*Proof.* Note that   $$ \frac{XJ}{IA}=\frac{YJ}{YA}=\frac{VU}{VI}=\frac{XU}{IA} \implies XJ=XU $$   as desired.  $\quad\square$ Secondly, 
<span style="color:#f00">**Claim.**</span> We have  $\overline{IX}\perp\overline{JU}$ *Proof.* Before this, we show that  $\{B,J,I,U\}$  are cyclic points. To see this, we observe that   $$ \angle BJU=\angle BAI=\angle BIU. $$   Now, since  $\overline{BI}$  is bisector of  $\angle ABC$  we have  $IJ=IU$ . Consequently,  $\overline{IX}\perp\overline{JU}$ , as desired.  $-(\clubsuit)\quad\square$ We also note that   $$ \angle BYI=\angle BAI=\angle BIU=\angle BIV. $$  Hence  $\{B,Y,I,V\}$  are cyclic points, as well  $-(\clubsuit\clubsuit)$ .
[rule]
To finish, let  $V'=\overline{VI}\cap \odot ABC \neq V$  and  $L=\overline{XU}\cap\overline{AV'}$ . Notice that   $$ \angle BYI=\angle BAI=\angle BIU=\angle BIV $$   Hence  $\{B,Y,I,V\}$  are cyclic points. By Reims, we have  $\overline{YI}\parallel\overline{AV'}$ . Next, we note   $$ 90^\circ=\angle IXU=\angle AIX=\angle IAV'=\angle ULV'. $$   So, it follows that  $V'$  is the midpoint of  $\widehat{BAC}$  and  $\{V',L,U,Z\}$  are cyclic points. Also, we observe that  $\{L,W,I\}$  are collinear. Finally, we let  $Z^*=\overline{LI}\cap\odot V'LU \neq L$ . By straightforward angle chasing it follows that   $$ \angle UV'Z=\tfrac{\angle C}{2}=\angle ULI $$   and   $$ \angle UV'Z=\angle ULI=\angle ULZ^*=\angle UV'Z^*, $$   which shows that  $Z\equiv Z^*$  i.e.  $\{W,I,Z\}$  are collinear, as desired.  $\blacksquare$ [rule]**Another finish, due to <span style="color:#00f">srijonrick</span>**:-
From  $(\clubsuit)$  and  $(\clubsuit\clubsuit)$ , we get  $V$  as the  $A$ -Mixtilinear touch point. It's well known that, if  $Q$  is the  $A$ -extouch point, then  $AV, AQ$  are Isogonals w.r.t  $\angle BAC$  (due to  $\sqrt{bc}$  inversion). That is,  $\angle BAV = \angle CAQ,$  so,  $\angle QAI=\angle VAI$ .

Now, since  $D$  and  $Q$  are the  $A$ -intouch point and  $A$ -extouch point respectively, hence by **Diameter of the Incircle lemma**  $BD=QC \implies ZD=ZQ$ . So,  $Z$  is the midpoint of  $DQ$ . Now, in  $\triangle ADQ$ , if we let  $N$  denote the midpoint of  $AD$ , we get  $NZ \parallel AQ.$  If  $D'$  is the antipode of  $D$  w.r.t  $\odot(I)$ , by the same lemma we have  $A, D', Q$  collinear. Now, by homothety at  $D$  with a ratio of  $-\tfrac12$ , we get  $A, D'$  and  $Q$  mapped to  $N, I$  and  $Z$  respectively, thus  $N, I, Z$  are collinear, and hence  $IZ \parallel AQ.$ At last, we observe that   $$ \measuredangle ZIA = \measuredangle IAQ = \measuredangle VAI = \measuredangle WAI = -\measuredangle AIW $$  (since  $\angle WAI = \angle WIA$ , as  $W$  is the midpoint of  $AX$  and we proved  $\angle AIY = 90^{\circ}$ ) so, we're done.
[rule]
