<span style="color:#00f">**CLAIM 1.**</span>  $VA=VC$ <span style="color:#00f">**Proof.**</span>
By Ceva's Theorem
\begin{align*}
1&=\frac{\sin\angle AIX}{\sin\angle XIV}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle IVY}{\sin\angle YVA}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle YAV}{\sin\angle YAI}
&=\frac{\sin\angle AIX}{\sin\angle XIV}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle AIV}{\sin\angle VAI}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle BAV}{\sin\angle BAM}
&=\frac{AX}{XV}\cdot\frac{VI}{AI}\cdot\frac{AV}{VI}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle BAV}{\sin\angle BAM}
&=\frac{UI}{UV}\cdot\frac{AV}{AI}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle BAV}{\sin\angle BAM}
&=\frac{\sin\angle UAM}{\sin\angle UAV}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle BAV}{\sin\angle BAM}
\end{align*}
Therefore, letting  $G=VI\cap AB$  and  $N$  be the midpoint of minor arc  $\widehat{AC}$ , we have  $$ -1=(AV,AI;AU,AB)\overset{A}{=}(V,I;U,G)\overset{B}{=}(V,N;C,A) $$  but  $NA=CN$ , hence  $VA=VC$  as well.  $\blacksquare$ <span style="color:#00f">**CLAIM 2.**</span> If  $P$  is the midpoint of  $AC$ , then  $\overline{YXP}\perp AI$ .
<span style="color:#00f">**Proof.**</span>
Firstly, let  $Q$  be the major arc midpoint of  $\widehat{BC}$ , then  $V,I,Q$  are collinear since  $VQ\perp CI$ .
Secondly, notice that  $VC$  is tangent to the circle centered at  $N$  with radius  $NC$ , hence by spiral sim. lemma,  $$ \triangle IVC\sim\triangle IPA $$  Hence  $\angle AIP=\angle IVC=90^{\circ}$ Therefore,  $P,V$  are the touching point of the  $A$ -mixtillinear circle with  $AC$  and  $(ABC)$  respectively. Let  $H$  be the minor arc midpoint of  $\widehat{AB}$ , suppose  $HV\cap AB=Y'$   $$ \angle BY'V=\angle ABH+\angle BCV=90^{\circ}-\frac{B}{2}-\frac{C}{2}=\frac{A}{2}=\angle BAI $$  hence  $Y'V\|AI$ , which implies  $Y'=Y$ . Therefore,  $Y$  is the touching point of  $A$ -mixtillinear circle with  $AB$ . Hence  $\overline{YIP}\perp AI$  as desired.  $\blacksquare$ Now  $IV$  is the  $I-$ symmedian in  $\triangle IBC$ .
Therefore, if  $M$  is the minor arc midpoint of  $\widehat BC$ , then  $$ \angle MIZ=\angle MIC-\angle CIZ=\frac{A}{2}+\frac{C}{2}-\frac{A}{2}=\frac{C}{2}=\angle ACI=\angle WAI=\angle WIA $$  as desired.

