A fair bit of potentially annoying technical configuration for rigor (much of which I include only to be general), but the actual solution is simple $^{[\text{A}]}$  (the entire idea is more or less contained in the section **Solution**):**Setup:** <details><summary>(Note about notation)</summary>In the interest of concision, the ordinal  $\alpha$  is here identified with the collection of ordinals preceding  $\alpha$ , e.g.: \begin{align*} 0&=\emptyset 2015&=\left\{0,\dots,2014\right\} \omega&=\left\{0,1,\dots,\right\}\end{align*} In particular,  $0$  is natural. For ordinals  $n_{0},n_{1}$ , the collection of maps from  $n_{0}$  to  $n_{1}$  is written  $n_{0}\to n_{1}$ .</details> Fix natural  $n\colon\omega$ ; we solve the more general problem of which  $n=2015$  is a subcase. Without loss of generality, incommutably *label* the  $n$  usamons by bijecting them to the elements of  $n$  and interpret "states" of the problem (where a "state" is the data of an electron configuration for each usamon) as equivalent to maps  $\chi\colon n\to 2$  in the obvious way. For each pair  $\left(l,r\right)\colon n\times n$  there is a map   $$ \sigma_{l,r}\colon\left(n\to 2\right)\to\left(n\to 2\right) $$   which manifests the action of connecting the diode from usamon  $l$  to usamon  $r$ ; concretely, it acts in accordance to the equations   $$ \sigma_{l,r}\left(\chi\right)\left(i\right)=\begin{cases}\text{min}\left(\chi\left(l\right),\chi\left(r\right)\right)\text{ if }i=l \text{max}\left(\chi\left(l\right),\chi\left(r\right)\right)\text{ if }i=r \chi\left(i\right)\text{ otherwise.}\end{cases} $$   For fixed labels  $\left(s,d\right)\colon n\times n$ , define an  $\left(n,s,d\right)$ *-swapgorithm*  $\Sigma$  to entail the data of \begin{align*} (\textbf{a})&\text{ A natural }t_{\Sigma}\colon\omega (\textbf{b})&\text{ A map }l_{\Sigma}\colon t_{\Sigma}\to n (\textbf{c})&\text{ A map }r_{\Sigma}\colon t_{\Sigma}\to n.\end{align*} In particular, we implement  $\Sigma$  as the algorithm in  $t_{\Sigma}$  stages which at stage  $i\colon t_{\Sigma}$  connects the diode from  $l_{\Sigma}\left(i\right)$  to  $r_{\Sigma}\left(i\right)$ —concretely, which acts on states as   $$ \sigma_{\Sigma}:=\sigma_{l_{\Sigma}\left(t-1\right),r_{\Sigma}\left(t-1\right)}\circ\cdots\circ\sigma_{l_{\Sigma}\left(0\right),r_{\Sigma}\left(0\right)}\colon\left(n\to 2\right)\to\left(n\to 2\right). $$   Let  $\text{const}_{n,s,d}$  be the subcollection of  $n\to 2$  spanning the maps  $\chi\colon n\to 2$  for whom  $\chi\left(s\right)=\chi\left(d\right)$ . We say that  $\Sigma$  is *successful* if after implementing  $\Sigma$  it is guaranteed that the usamon labeled  $s$  and the usamon labeled  $d$  are isoelectronic—or, concretely, that   $$ \forall\chi\colon n\to 2,\ \sigma_{\Sigma}\left(\chi\right)\colon\text{const}_{n,s,d} $$   —and denote the collection of successful  $\left(n,s,d\right)$ -swapgorithms  $\left(n,s,d\right)\textbf{-SuccS}$ . **Lemma (Transport of successful swapgorithms $^{\text{[\text{B}]}}$ ):** Given naturals  $n\colon\omega$  and  $n'\colon\omega$ , pairs  $\left(s,d\right)\colon n\times n$  and  $\left(s',d'\right)\colon n'\times n'$ , and map  $\varphi\colon n\to n'$  preserving  $s$  and  $d$  in the sense that  $s'=\varphi\left(s\right)$  and  $d'=\varphi\left(d\right)$ , there is a map   $$ \textbf{SuccS}\left(\varphi\right)\colon\left(n,s,d\right)\textbf{-SuccS}\to \left(n',s',d'
\right)\textbf{-SuccS} $$   computed as \begin{align*} (\textbf{a})&\ t_{\textbf{SuccS}\left(\varphi\right)\left(\Sigma\right)}=t_{\Sigma} (\textbf{b})&\ l_{\textbf{SuccS}\left(\varphi\right)\left(\Sigma\right)}=\varphi\circ l_{\Sigma} (\textbf{c})&\ r_{\textbf{SuccS}\left(\varphi\right)\left(\Sigma\right)}=\varphi\circ r_{\Sigma}.\end{align*}**Proof:** $^{\text{[\text{C}]}}$  Consider the natural map  $-\circ\varphi\colon\left(n'\to 2\right)\to\left(n\to 2\right)$  given by precomposing  $\varphi$ . The critical property is that  $-\circ\varphi$  *reflects* the  $\text{const}$  construction in the sense that   $$ \chi\circ\varphi\colon\text{const}_{n,s,d}\implies\chi\colon\text{const}_{n',s',d'}. $$   (In fact, it also *preserves* the construction in the sense of the converse.) It is moreover not too difficultly seen that for  $\left(l,r\right)\colon n\times n$  and arbitrary  $\chi\colon n'\to 2$ ,  $i\colon n'$ ,   $$ \left(-\circ\varphi\right)\circ\sigma_{\varphi\left(l\right),\varphi\left(r\right)}\left(\chi\right)\left(i\right)=\begin{cases}\text{min}\left(\chi\left(\varphi\left(l\right)\right),\chi\left(\varphi\left(r\right)\right)\right)\text{ if }i=l \text{max}\left(\chi\left(\varphi\left(l\right)\right),\chi\left(\varphi\left(r\right)\right)\right)\text{ if }i=r \chi\left(\varphi\left(i\right)\right)\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}=\sigma_{l,r}\circ\left(-\circ\varphi\right)\left(\chi\right)\left(i\right). $$   Whence   $$ \left(-\circ\varphi\right)\circ\sigma_{\varphi\left(l\right),\varphi\left(r\right)}=\sigma_{l,r}\circ\left(-\circ\varphi\right) $$   as maps  $\left(n'\to 2\right)\to\left(n\to 2\right)$  (which is perhaps more intuitively expressed in the form of a diagram, but alas). It follows (by constructing the obvious "big" diagram) that \begin{align*}\left(-\circ\varphi\right)\circ\sigma_{\textbf{SuccS}\left(\varphi\right)\left(\Sigma\right)}&=\left(-\circ\varphi\right)\circ\sigma_{\varphi\left(l_{\Sigma}\left(t_{\Sigma}-1\right)\right),\varphi\left(r_{\Sigma}\left(t_{\Sigma}-1\right)\right)}\circ\cdots\circ\sigma_{\varphi\left(l_{\Sigma}\left(0\right)\right),\varphi\left(r_{\Sigma}\left(0\right)\right)} &=\sigma_{l_{\Sigma}\left(t_{\Sigma}-1\right),r_{\Sigma}\left(t_{\Sigma}-1\right)}\circ\left(-\circ\varphi\right)\circ\cdots\circ\sigma_{\varphi\left(l_{\Sigma}\left(0\right)\right),\varphi\left(r_{\Sigma}\left(0\right)\right)} &\cdots &=\sigma_{l_{\Sigma}\left(t_{\Sigma}-1\right),r_{\Sigma}\left(t_{\Sigma}-1\right)}\circ\cdots\circ\left(-\circ\varphi\right)\circ\sigma_{\varphi\left(l_{\Sigma}\left(0\right)\right),\varphi\left(r_{\Sigma}\left(0\right)\right)} &=\sigma_{l\left(t_{\Sigma}-1\right),r\left(t_\Sigma-1\right)}\circ\cdots\circ\sigma_{l\left(0\right),r\left(0\right)}\circ\left(-\circ\varphi\right) &=\sigma_{\Sigma}\circ\left(-\circ\varphi\right)\end{align*} as maps  $\left(n'\to 2\right)\to\left(n\to 2\right)$ . We deduce, by the premise of  $\Sigma$ 's success, that  $\forall\chi\colon n'\to 2$ , \begin{align*}\sigma_{\Sigma}\left(\chi\circ\varphi\right)\colon\text{const}_{n,s,d}&\implies\sigma_{\Sigma}\circ\left(-\circ\varphi\right)\left(\chi\right)\colon\text{const}_{n,s,d} &\implies\left(-\circ\varphi\right)\circ\sigma_{\textbf{SuccS}\left(\varphi\right)\left(\Sigma\right)}\left(\chi\right)\colon\text{const}_{n,s,d} &\implies\sigma_{\textbf{SuccS}\left(\varphi\right)\left(\Sigma\right)}\left(\chi\right)\circ\varphi\colon\text{const}_{n,s,d} &\implies\sigma_{\textbf{SuccS}\left(\varphi\right)\left(\Sigma\right)}\left(\chi\right)\colon\text{const}_{n',s',d'}.\end{align*} I.e., that  $\textbf{SuccS}\left(\varphi\right)\left(\Sigma\right)$  is successful.  $\Box$ **Solution:** It suffices to show that  $\left(2015,0,1\right)\textbf{-SuccS}$  is empty $^{\text{[\text{D}]}}$ . But the map  $2015\to 2$  that bundles  $\left\{0\right\}$  over  $0$  and  $\left\{1,\dots,2014\right\}$  over  $1$  via the **Lemma** renders a map  $\left(2015,0,1\right)\textbf{-SuccS}\to \left(2,0,1\right)\textbf{-SuccS}$ , and the latter is clearly empty (If the usamon labeled  $0$  and that labeled  $1$  are not isoelectronic at the start of the problem, the physicist stands no chance.), whence the former is too.  $\blacksquare$ -----
[center]**Footnotes:**[/center]
\begin{align*}
\text{[\text{A}]}&\text{ Actually, it's remarkably similar to }\textbf{u/lokitos}\text{'s (and others') solution(s), but instead of approaching the problem from the perspective of ``Really, } &\text{ there's not much at all that the physicist }\textit{can}\text{ know besides relative order.''---which is at least to me a very natural motivation!---it motivates by } &\text{ asking ``It is clearly impossible to succeed with }2\text{ usamons; na\"ively with }2015\text{ shouldn't be any easier---can I turn a guaranteed success with }2015\text{ } &\text{ usamons into a guaranteed success with }2\text{? Sure, let me just use those I have multiplicitously in such a way that I don't end up declaring a usamon } &\text{ isoelectronic to itself.'' } \text{[\text{B}]}&\text{ Alternatively, ``}\textbf{SuccS}\text{ is functorial''. } \text{[\text{C}]}&\text{ The claim itself is fairly intuitive, and the technical proof given here is but in the interest of completeness. } \text{[\text{D}]}&\text{ This is because the physicist might as well identity the usamons she will eventually claim isoelectronic before doing anything else (even labeling; } &\text{ certainly she receives no output from the system, and thus the problem is invariant to permutation of the usamons' labels), and because there is no } & \text{ (mathematical) harm in allowing that the physicist connect the diode from usamons to themselves.}\end{align*}

Here is a solution from the Thailand TST group's live solve.**First part:** We will show a slightly stronger bound, which will pay off in the second part.

<span style="color:red">**Claim:**</span>  $x<1.8$ *Proof:* Eliminate  $y$  and  $z$  by the following bound.
\begin{align*}
    x^4+y^4+z^4+xyz &\geq x^4+2y^2z^2+xyz 
    &= x^4 + 2\left(yz+\frac x4\right)^2 - \frac{x^2}{8}
    &\geq x^4-\frac{x^2}{8}
\end{align*}
Thus  $8x^4-x^2\leq 32$ , which is equivalent to  $|x|\leq\frac{\sqrt{1+5\sqrt{41}}}{4}<1.8$ .  $\blacksquare$ **Second part:** Assume for the contradiction that  $(y+z)^2>8-4x$ . We claim the following bound.

<span style="color:red">**Claim:**</span>  $y^4+z^4+xyz\geq \dfrac{(y+z)^4+2x(y+z)^2}{8}$ *Proof:* Multiply both sides by  $8$  and note the following identity.
\begin{align*}8(y^4+z^4)-(y+z)^4 &= (y-z)^2(7y^2+10yz+7z^2) 
&= 6(y+z)^2(y-z)^2 + (y-z)^4 
&\geq (48-24x)(y-z)^2\end{align*}
Thus we have  $$ 8(y^4+z^4+xyz)\geq (48-24x)(y-z)^2 - 2x(y-z)^2 = (48-26x)(y-z)^2\geq 0 $$  as claimed.  $\blacksquare$ Now it's easy to finish. Keeping in mind that  $(y+z)^2+x > 8-3x\geq 0$ , we bound
\begin{align*}
x^4+y^4+z^4+xyz &\geq x^4+\dfrac{(y+z)^4+2x(y+z)^2}{8} 
&= x^4+\dfrac{((y+z)^2+x)^2-x^2}{8} 
&> x^4+\dfrac{(8-3x)^2-x^2}{8} 
&= x^4+x^2-6x+8
\end{align*}
Thus we have  $x^4+x^2-6x+4 <0$ , which rearranges to  $(x-1)^2(x^2+2x+4)<0$ , contradiction.

solution_md:
Name the players Alice and Bob in that order. We view the problem with elements of the tuple as named variables (all zero-initialized except for one) and elements of  $S$  as named "functions" acting on the variables. To differentiate, we will refer to variables in capital letters and functions in lowercase. We will name the first tuple element  $\text{NUM}(1)$  and define the rest as we go. If not specifically specified, a function does not change the values of a given variable. Finally, all inputs are to be taken modulo how many possible numbers they can equal, except for in the definition of  $\text{beginkill}(i,j)$ .

Our first two moves will be unique, setting up the rest of the process. We define the variables  $\text{INIT}(1),\text{INIT}(2),\text{INIT}(3)$  along with  $\text{TURN}(1),\text{TURN}(2)$ . We define functions  $\text{begin}(1),\text{begin}(2)$  and  $\text{beginkill}(i,j)$  for each  $(i,j) \in \{1,2\} \times \{1,2,3\}$  as follows:


- $\text{begin}(1)$  decreases  $\text{NUM}(1)$  by  $1$  and increases all  $\text{INIT}(i)$  by  $1$ .
- $\text{begin}(2)$  increases  $\text{NUM}(1)$  by  $1$ , decreases  $\text{INIT}(1)$  by  $1$ , and increases  $\text{TURN}(1)$  by  $1$ .
- $\text{beginkill}(i,j)$  decreases  $\text{INIT}(1)$  and  $\text{INIT}(i+1)$  by  $2$  and decreases  $\text{TURN}(j)$  by  $1$  (if  $j=3$  then nothing happens for the last part).
[/list]
We now split the rest of  $S$  into two sets of functions  $A,B$  such that:
[list]
- Every function in  $A$  doesn't change any  $\text{INIT}(i)$ , decreases  $\text{TURN}(1)$  by  $1$ , and increases  $\text{TURN}(2)$  by  $1$ .
- Every function in  $B$  doesn't change any  $\text{INIT}(i)$ , increases  $\text{TURN}(1)$  by  $1$ , and decreases  $\text{TURN}(2)$  by  $1$ .
[/list]
Clearly the first two moves must be  $\text{begin}(1)$  and  $\text{begin}(2)$  in that order. Now suppose that  $\text{begin}(1)$  is called at some later point; clearly we cannot have called  $\text{begin}(2)$  (except at the start) or any  $\text{beginkill}(i,j)$  before this. When this happens both  $\text{INIT}(2)$  and  $\text{INIT}(3)$  become  $2$ , and the other player can call some  $\text{beginkill}(1,j)$  with  $j \in \{1,2\}$  (based on player), which makes  $\text{TURN}(1)=\text{TURN}(2)=\text{INIT}(1)=0$ . After this, the only option left for the original caller of  $\text{begin}(1)$  is to call  $\text{begin}(1)$  again, and the other player can call  $\text{beginkill}(2,3)$ . Then this repeats, with the other player alternating between  $\text{beginkill}(1,3)$  and  $\text{beginkill}(2,3)$  until the original  $\text{begin}(1)$  caller decreases  $\text{NUM}(1)$  to negative.

Hence calling  $\text{begin}(1)$  after the start is a guaranteed loss, so  $\text{begin}(2)$  and  $\text{beginkill}(i,j)$  will not be called after the first two turns. In particular, for the rest of the game Alice will always call functions from  $A$  and Bob will always call functions from  $B$ . We set up the rest of the functions and variables now.

Along with  $\text{NUM}(1)$ , define  $\text{NUM}(2)$  and  $\text{NUM}(3)$ . Then for each  $i \in \{1,2,3\}$  and  $j \in \{1,2\}$  we define  $\text{STATE}(i,j)$ . Finally, we define the variable  $\text{WIN}$ . We modify  $\text{begin}(2)$  to also initialize  $\text{STATE}(1,1)$  to  $1$ .

The idea is that during normal gameplay exactly one of these will equal  $1$ , and the rest  $0$ . If  $\text{STATE}(i,j)$  is currently  $1$ , then the current player is  $j$  and the value of  $i$  reflects the fact that  $\text{NUM}(i)$  is "active". During player  $j$ 's turn, we normally (i.e. unless otherwise stated) decrement some  $\text{STATE}(i_1,j)$  and increment some  $\text{STATE}(i_2,3-j)$  ( $i_1=i_2$  is allowed). For convenience we will define a tracker  $\text{CUR} \in \{1,2,3\}$  (with values taken modulo  $3$ , so  $0=3$ ), corresponding to the value of  $i$  such that  $\text{STATE}(i,j)=1$ ; note that this is not a tuple element. In some situations we will change (i.e. decrement) the only  $\text{STATE}(i,j)$  equal to  $1$  to  $0$ . In this case we let  $\text{CUR}=\text{null}$ ; note that this requires  $\text{CUR} \neq \text{NULL}$  to begin with. This therefore has the effect of depriving the other player of any moves, making them instantly lose.

Now we define functions for Alice; the modification of  $\text{STATE}(i,j)$  is omitted and we instead provide a description of how  $\text{CUR}$  changes:
[list]
- $\text{basic}()$  will keep  $\text{CUR}$  and decrease  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})$  by  $1$ . To be explicit, we actually have three functions  $\text{basic}(i)$  for  $i \in \{1,2,3\}$  that decrease  $\text{NUM}(i)$  by  $1$ , decrease  $\text{STATE}(i,1)$  by  $1$ , and increase  $\text{STATE}(i,2)$  by  $1$ . Thus note that "maintaining"  $\text{CUR}$  actually means there is a decrement and an increment as usual, despite the fact that it sounds like nothing is happening to the  $\text{STATE}$  variables. Since we can only ever run  $\text{basic}(\text{CUR})$ , for convenience we just refer to all three with  $\text{basic}()$ .
- $\text{advance}()$  will increase  $\text{CUR}$  by  $1$ , then decrease  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})$  by  $1$ .
- $\text{claimwin}()$  will maintain  $\text{CUR}$ , decrease  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})$  by  $1$ , and increase  $\text{WIN}$  by  $1$  (hence we cannot have  $\text{CUR}=\text{null})$ .
- $\text{claimkill}()$  will decrease  $\text{WIN}$  by  $1$ , decrease  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR}-1)$  by  $1$  and then set  $\text{CUR}=\text{null}$ .
[/list]
Here are functions for Bob:
[list]
- $\text{basic}()$  will decrease  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})$  by  $1$  and increase  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR}+1)$  by  $1$ .
- $\text{advancekill}()$  will decrease  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR}-1)$  by  $1$  and then set  $\text{CUR}=\text{null}$ .
- $\text{claimwin}()$  will increase  $\text{WIN}$  by  $1$ , increase  $\text{CUR}$  by  $1$ , and then decrease  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})$  by  $1$ .
- $\text{claimkill}()$  will actually refer to three separate "functions" (although each "function" corresponds to  $3$  *actual* functions based on  $\text{CUR})$  that for  $i \in \{1,2,3\}$ , decrease  $\text{WIN}$  by  $1$ , decrease  $\text{NUM}(i)$  by  $1$ , and set  $\text{CUR}=\text{null}$ .
[/list]

Note that:
[list]
- Alice cannot call  $\text{advance}()$  unless  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})=0$ , else Bob wins with  $\text{advancekill}()$ . Likewise, Bob can only call  $\text{advancekill}()$  after Alice improperly calls  $\text{advance}()$ .
- Alice cannot call  $\text{claimwin}()$  unless we have  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})=1$  and the other two  $\text{NUM}$  variables  $0$ , else Bob can call some  $\text{claimkill}()$  and win. However, if she validly calls  $\text{claimwin}()$  then Bob has no moves and loses. Finally Bob clearly cannot call  $\text{claimkill}()$  unless Alice calls  $\text{claimwin}()$  first.
- Bob cannot call  $\text{claimwin}()$  unless we have  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})=0$  (with  $\text{CUR}$  defined pre-increment) and  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR}+1)>0$ ; the latter is necessary to run it in the first place, but if the former doesn't hold then Alice can call  $\text{claimkill}()$  to win. Finally Alice clearly cannot call  $\text{claimkill}()$  unless Bob calls  $\text{claimwin}()$  first.
[/list]

Thus all moves are actually fixed, and proceed as follows:
[list]
- While  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR}) \geq 2$ , Alice and Bob must call  $\text{basic}()$  once each and repeat. This increases  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR}+1)$  by  $1$ .
- If during Alice's turn we have  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})=0$ , then she must call  $\text{advance}()$ . When this happens, the previous value of  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})$  (i.e. before we started performing the previous step with the old value of  $\text{CUR}$ ) will have been even, and for a brief moment mid-function call our new value of  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})$  (with the new value of  $\text{CUR}$ ) will be exactly half of the previous one. Then it decreases by  $1$  in the second half of the function call, so we essentially start over with the value of  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})$  exactly halved.
- If during Alice's turn we have  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})=1$  and  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})>0$ , then it must have "originally" (i.e. right after  $\text{CUR}$  was last changed to its current value) been odd and not  $1$ , i.e.  $n$  would not have been a power of  $2$ . Then Alice is still forced to call  $\text{basic}()$ , but now Bob can run  $\text{claimwin}()$  and win.
- If instead during Alice's turn we have  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})=1$  but  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})=0$ , then  $n$  was originally a power of  $2$ . Although Alice is still forced to call  $\text{basic}()$ , Bob can no longer run  $\text{claimwin}()$ ; nor can he call anything else, so he loses.

This finishes the problem.  $\blacksquare$ **Remark:** The  $\text{STATE}(\bullet,j)$  variables essentially mirror  $\text{TURN}(j)$  at all times, so the  $\text{TURN}(j)$  existing might not be necessary. However, I think it's easier to think about these two parts of our "program" as separate.**Remark:** Our final program can be viewed as an implementation of the following pseudocode:

```

int n;
int q = 0;

while (true) {
    if (n >= 2) {
        n -= 2;
        q++;
    } else if (n == 1) {
        if (q == 0) EndAliceWin();
        else EndBobWin();
    } else if (n == 0) {
        n = q;
        q = 0;
    };
};

```
I mentioned this in the writeup, but it's quite confusing so I'll repeat it again: in the  $n=0$  case, our while loop actually ends with the incrementing of  $\text{CUR}$  in  $\text{advance}()$ , and the next iteration of the loop begins with the decrementing of  $\text{NUM}(\text{CUR})$ .

