By symmetry, it is enough to show that  $X_a$  lies on  $(HG)$ . By angle chasing and radical axis, this is equivalent to showing that  $\mathrm{Pow}_{(AH)}M_a=\mathrm{Pow}_{(L_aAK_a)}M$ .**Claim:  $\mathrm{Pow}_{(L_aAK_a)}M=M_aB^2$**

*Proof:*  $(L_aK_a;BC)$  is harmonic by the right angles and angle bisectors lemma. Hence by the self polar orthogonality lemma,  $(BC)$  is orthogonal to  $(L_aAK_a)$ . Consequently  $\mathrm{Pow}_{(L_aAK_a)}=M_aB\cdot M_aC=M_aB^2$  as claimed. $\square$ **Claim:  $\mathrm{Pow}_{(AH)}M=M_aB^2$**

*Proof:* It is well known that  $BCEF$  is cyclic. Hence by Brocard's theorem  $A$  lies on the polar of  $H$ . Hence by the self polar orthogonality lemma, we have the desired conclusion. $\square$ Hence the problem is solved.

solution_md:
We use the common trick of letting  $a=x+y$  and  $b=x-y$ . Note that  $x,y$  have the same parity. Since  $3(x+y)^4+(x-y)^4=4x^2+4xy+4y^2$ , we can rewrite the equation as:  $$ 3a^2+b^2=4\left(\frac{a}{3}+1\right)^3 $$  This implies that  $a$  is divisible by  $3$ , so let  $a=3c$  where  $c$  is also an integer. Then the equation becomes:
\begin{align*}
27c^2+b^2&=4(c+1)^3
b^2&=4c^3-15c^2+12c+4
\end{align*}
We try to see if we can factor the LHS of this equation. After some trial and error, we find that  $(c-2)$  is a factor. Using synthetic division, we find that  $4c^3-15c^2+12c+4=(c-2)(4c^2-7c-2)$ . Factoring the quadratic is straightforward. After doing so, we are amazingly left with:  $$ b^2=(c-2)^2(4c+1) $$  Which implies that  $4c+1$  is a perfect square. For parity reasons, it must be the square of an odd integer. Therefore, for an integer  $n$ , we can write:
\begin{align*}
4c+1&=(2n+1)^2
c&=n^2+n
\end{align*}
Then, after substituting, we get that  $a=3n^2+3n$  and  $b^2=(n^2+n-2)^2(2n+1)^2 \implies b=\pm (n^2+n-2)(2n+1)$ . We will take the  $+$  value, since the  $-$  value just swaps  $x$  and  $y$ . This makes  $b=2n^3+3n^2-3n-2$ .
Noting that  $x=\frac{a+b}{2}$  and  $y=\frac{a-b}{2}$ , we substitute to get:  $$ \boxed{(x,y)=(n^3+3n^2-1,-n^3+3n+1)} $$  And permutations. If necessary, we can check these values by plugging them into the equation again, and see that they all work.  $\blacksquare$

candidates:
The equation rewrites into \[(x+y)^2+y^2-(x+y)y=\left(\frac{x+y}{3}+1\right)^3\] Then as  $3\mid x+y$  we have  $x+y=3a$  for some  $a\in \mathbb{Z}$ , hence substituting and simplifying gives \[y^2-3ay+6a^2-a^3-3a-1=0.\] So we need  $\Delta_y=\Box$  (perfect square), that is \[9a^2-4(6a^2-a^3-3a-1)=(a-2)^2(4a+1)=\Box.\] Therefore  $4a+1=u^2$ , which is odd so  $u=2v+1$  giving  $4a+1=4v^2+4v+1$  which simplifies to  $a=v(v+1)$  (note:  $a/2$  is a triangular number!). Then we have  $\Delta_y$   $=(a-2)^2(4a+1)$   $=((v(v+1)-2)(2v+1))^2$  so solving the previous quadratic in  $y$  gives
\begin{align*}
y&=\frac{3v(v+1)\pm (v(v+1)-2)(2v+1)}{2}
&\in \{v^3+3v^2-1,-v^3+3v+1\}.
\end{align*}
Then  $x+y=3a=3v(v+1)$  gives \[x\in \{-v^3+3v+1,v^3+3v^2-1\}.\]
So the solution is \[(x,y)\ \text{or}\ (y,x)=(v^3+3v^2-1,-v^3+3v+1).\]

Let  $x+y=3a$  and  $b=xy$ . Then  $9a^2-b=(a+1)^3$ , so  $b=-a^3+6a^2-3a-1$ . But  $b=x(3a-x)$ , so 
\begin{align*}
x(3a-x) = -a^3+6a^2-3a-1 &\implies x^2-3ax-(a^3-6a^2+3a+1)=0 
&\implies x = \frac{3a \pm \sqrt{9a^2+4(a^3-6a^2+3a+1)}}{2}
&\qquad \qquad = \frac{3a\pm (a-2)\sqrt{4a+1}}{2}.
\end{align*} 
So  $4a+1=n^2$  for some  $n$ . But since  $n$  is odd, we can write  $n=2m+1$ , so  $a=m^2+m$ . So 
\begin{align*}
x &= \frac{3(m^2+m) \pm (m^2+m-2)(2m+1)}{2}, \ y= \frac{3(m^2+m) \mp (m^2+m-2)(2m+1)}{2} 
&\implies (x,y)=(m^3+3m^2-1,-m^3+3m+1),
\end{align*}
or vice-versa. 

Remark: My solution to this is very similar to my solution of ISL 2014 N2.

Since the LHS is an integer, we must have  $3 \mid x+y.$  Letting  $x+y=3a, x-y=b,$  we have 
\begin{align*}
27a^2+b^2=4(a+1)^3 &\iff b^2=4a^3-15a^2+12a+4 
&\iff b^2=(a-2)^2(4a+1) 
&\implies 4a+1 \ \text{is a perfect square.} 
\end{align*}
Thus, we must have  $a=n^2+n$  where  $n \in \mathbb{Z}.$  Plugging this back in, we find that the answer is
\[ (x,y)=\boxed{(n^3+3n^2-1, -n^3+3n+1)}\]
and its permutations.  $\blacksquare$

solution_md:
Our synthetic solution follows with a series of claims.

<span style="color:#00f">**Claim 1:**</span>  $\triangle APX \sim \triangle ASP$ .

We have  $\angle PAX = \angle SAP$ , and 
\begin{align*}
\angle APX &= \angle APQ = \angle ABQ = \angle ABP = \angle ASP \text{ } \Box 
&\implies \boxed{AP^2 = AX \cdot AS.}
\end{align*}

<span style="color:#00f">**Claim 2:**</span> The length  $AN$  is fixed.

Denote  $Y$  as the midpoint of  $AS$ , implying  $(XYM)$  is the nine-point circle of  $\triangle AST$ . Then let  $H$  and  $N$  be the orthocenter and nine-point center of  $\triangle AST$ , respectively.

Using Power of a Point, we find 
\begin{align*}
AN^2 &= pow(A, (XYM)) + R^2_{(XYM)} 
&= AX \cdot AY + \frac{1}{4} R^2_{(APBQ)} 
&= \frac{1}{2} AP^2 + \frac{1}{4} R^2_{(APBQ)},
\end{align*}
which is a fixed quantity.  $\Box$ <span style="color:#00f">**Claim 3:**</span>  $AN = KM$ , where  $K$  is the midpoint of  $AO$ .

This follows from a stronger statement -  $AKMN$  is a parallelogram. We have  $AK = NM$  as  $R_{(APBQ)} = 2R_{(XYM)}$ , and  $AK \parallel NM$  due to a homothety at  $H$  with ratio  $2$ .  $\Box$ Hence  $KM$  is a fixed quantity, meaning  $M$  moves along a circle centered at  $K$ .  $\blacksquare$ [asy]
size(256);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.4)+fontsize(8));

pair O, K, A, P, B, Q, S, X;
O = 0;
K = -.5;
A = -1;
P = dir(135);
B = 1;
Q = dir(225);
S = dir(40);
X = extension(A, S, P, Q);

pair [] T=intersectionpoints(circle(O, 1), 3X-2*(rotate(90, X)*S)--rotate(90, X)*S);

pair M, Y, H, N;
M = .5S + .5T[1];
Y = .5S + .5A;
H = orthocenter(A, S, T[1]);
N = .5H;

fill(A--P--S--cycle, palegreen);
fill(A--K--M--N--cycle, lightyellow);

draw(circle(O, 1));
draw(A--P--B--Q--A--P--Q);
draw(S--A--B);
draw(A--T[1]--S);
draw(T[1]--X);
draw(circumcircle(X, Y, M), blue);
draw(A--H--B, dashed);
draw(2N-M--M);
draw(P--S);
draw(A--N^^M--K);

label(" $O$ ", O, dir(90));
label(" $A$ ", A, W);
label(" $P$ ", P, NW);
label(" $B$ ", B, E);
label(" $Q$ ", Q, SW);
label(" $S$ ", S, NE);
label(" $X$ ", X, NW);
label(" $T$ ", T[1], dir(270));
label(" $Y$ ", Y, dir(90));
label(" $H$ ", H, dir(225));
label(" $N$ ", N, SE);
label(" $M$ ", M, dir(0));
label(" $K$ ", K, dir(90));

dot(O);
dot(A);
dot(P);
dot(B);
dot(Q);
dot(S);
dot(X);
dot(T[1]);
dot(Y);
dot(H);
dot(N);
dot(M);
dot(2N-M);
dot(K);
[/asy]

