Wow, the point  $B$  is a red herring.
<blockquote>Quadrilateral  $APBQ$  is inscribed in circle  $\omega$  with  $\angle P = \angle Q = 90^{\circ}$  and  $AP = AQ < BP$ . Let  $X$  be a variable point on segment  $\overline{PQ}$ . Line  $AX$  meets  $\omega$  again at  $S$  (other than  $A$ ). Point  $T$  lies on arc  $AQB$  of  $\omega$  such that  $\overline{XT}$  is perpendicular to  $\overline{AX}$ . Let  $M$  denote the midpoint of chord  $\overline{ST}$ . As  $X$  varies on segment  $\overline{PQ}$ , show that  $M$  moves along a circle.</blockquote>
Let  $U$  be the midpoint of  $AO.$  We claim that the locus lies on  $\mathcal{C}(U,UP).$  The key idea is to focus on  $\triangle AST.$  Let  $K$  be the foot of the  $A-\text{altitude}$  in  $\triangle AST$  and  $Y=PQ \cap AT.$  Then by the shooting lemma  $XYTS$  is cyclic and hence  $SY \perp AT.$ We claim that  $PQKM$  is cyclic. One way to see this is to join  $PQ \cap ST=V,$  and so  $VP \cdot VQ=VS \cdot VT$   $=VX \cdot VY=VK \cdot VM,$  where the last part follows since  $XYMK$  is the nine-point circle.  

So it suffices to show that  $UK=UM. \text{ }$  Let  $N$  be the nine-point center. Translate the figure so that  $O$  is the origin. Then  $$ \overrightarrow{UN} \cdot \overrightarrow{ST}=\left( \frac{\vec{a}+\vec{s}+\vec{t}}{2}-\frac{\vec{a}}{2} \right) \cdot (\vec{t}-\vec{s})=0 $$  Hence  $UN \perp ST$  and we are done.  $\square$ **P.S.** Another way to show  $UN \perp ST$  is note that  $UN$  is the  $O-\text{midline}$  in triangle  $OAH,$  and so  $UN \parallel AH \perp ST.$ **Remark:** Guessing the circle is not terribly hard if you are clever enough. Consider the edge-cases when  $X=P, Q$  and the midpoint of  $PQ.$  This helps us guess the circle. Next, a neat diagram shows that the center of this circle is the midpoint of  $AO,$  and it is not hard to prove that these two guesses are consistent, after which you get more confidence on your claim (not to mention drawing and checking for random points  $X)$ [asy]
