It suffices to show that if  $Y$  exists, then  $(B,D;A,C)=-1$ , since the result of the question would hold from symmetry. $\angle YAD=\angle CBD=\angle YBA$ , so  $(ABY)$  is tangent to  $\overline{AD}$  at  $A$ . Similarly,  $(ADY)$  touches  $\overline{AB}$  at  $A$ . Let the centers of these two circles be  $E,F$  respectively. 

If  $O$  is the circumcenter of  $ABCD$ , then  $FA\perp AB\perp EO$  and  $EA\perp AD\perp FO$  so  $AEOF$  is a parallelogram. Then  $EF$  passes through the midpoints of  $\overline{AO},\overline{AY}$ , hence  $EF\parallel OY$ , and  $OY\perp AC$ . Thus  $Y$  is the midpoint of  $\overline{AC}$ .

Then  $BD$  is the  $B$ -symmedian of  $\triangle ABC$ , so  $(B,D;A,C)=-1$  as required.

