We set up an equation in  $n$ , and then solve. 
<details><summary>Solution</summary>So, what is the probability of getting  $2$  heads out of  $n$  coin flips in terms of  $n$ ? Well, there are  $\binom{n}{2}$  ways to choose which two flips end up heads. For each of these  $\binom{n}{2}$  flips, we must have  $2$  heads and  $n - 2$  tails. Therefore, each of these  $\binom{n}{2}$  flips has probability  $\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^2\left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^{n - 2}.$  Hence, the probability of getting exactly  $2$  heads out of  $n$  coin flips is  $\binom{n}{2}\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^2\left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^{n - 2}.$  By using similar reasoning, we deduce that the probability of getting  $3$  heads out of  $n$  coin flips is  $\binom{n}{3}\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^3\left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^{n - 3}.$  

Setting equality, we deduce that 
\begin{align*}
\frac{n(n - 1)}{2} \cdot \left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^2 \cdot \left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^{n - 2} &= \frac{n(n - 1)(n - 2)}{6} \cdot \left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^3 \cdot \left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^{n - 3} 
\implies \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{3}{4} &= \frac{n - 2}{6} \cdot \frac{1}{4} 
\implies 9 &= n - 2.
\end{align*}
Therefore, our answer is  $n = \boxed{\textbf{(D)} \; 11}.$</details>

The probability  $p$  that Larry wins the game is the sum of probabilities that Larry wins on his first, second, third,  $\ldots$  throws. That is,
\begin{align*}
    p&=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{8}+\frac{1}{32}+\cdots.
\end{align*}
Using the close form of an infinite geometric series: \[\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}ar^{n-1}=\frac{a}{1-r}\] where  $a$  is the first term, and  $r$  is the common ratio, we obtain
\begin{align*}
    p&=\frac{\frac{1}{2}}{1-\frac{1}{4}} 
    &=\frac{2}{3}.
\end{align*}
