First, note that by the tangency  $\angle ADC=180-\angle ABC=\angle BYC$  and similarly  $\angle BCD=\angle AYD$ . Also,  $\angle ABY=\angle BCY$  and  $\angle BAY=\angle ADY$ . Combining all of this tells us that  $ADY$ ,  $BYC$ , and  $YAB$  are all similar to each other. Furthermore the ratio of similarity of  $ADY$  to  $YAB$  is  $AY:YB$  and the ratio of similarity of  $YAB$  to  $BYC$  is  $AY:YB$ . Hence, the triangles corresponding side lengths are in geometric progression so  $AB^2=CY\cdot DY$ .

Now we claim that  $CY\cdot DY=CX\cdot DX-XY^2$ . Then it will immediately follow that the answer is  $37\cdot67-47^2=270$ . To prove this, note that  $\angle DAY=\angle DXY$  and  $\angle YBC=\angle YXC$ , so  $XY$  is the angle bisector of  $\angle CXD$ . Now let  $I$  be the incenter of  $CXD$  and  $M$  be the midpoint of arc  $CD$ . Let  $\omega$  be the circumcircle of  $BIC$  and let its radius be  $R$ . Its circumcenter is  $M$ , so if it intersects  $XC$  again at  $D'$  then  $XD'=XD$  by symmetry. Also, the circumcircle of  $XYD$  is tangent to  $MD$  because  $\angle DXY=\angle CXM=\angle CDM$ . But now we know that  $CX\cdot DX-CY\cdot DY=CX\cdot D'X-CY\cdot DY=p(X,\omega)+p(Y,\omega)$  $=XM^2-R^2+YM^2-R^2=XM^2+YM^2-2MD^2$  $=XM^2+YM^2-2MX\cdot MY=XY^2$ , so we are done.</details>

