<details><summary>Probabilistic Recursive Solution</summary>For the ease of calling names, let the topmost ring be called ring 1 and the one to the right of it(counter-clockwise direction) be ring 2 and the one after that ring 3 and so on. Let's try finding the probability of ring 5 being the same color as ring 1. Call the probability that ring k is the same color as ring 1  $P(k)\forall 2\le k\le 5$ . Given any ring k, the  $P(k)$  is the same as the probability of ring k-1 being different from ring 1*1/3 because ring k has 3 choices and only 1 of those ends up the same as ring 1. So we get  $P(k)=\frac{1-P(k-1)}{3}$ . Obviously,  $P(2)=0$  as the first and second ring have to be of different colors. We eventually get that  $P(5)=\frac{7}{27}$ . Now, ring 1 has 4 choices and rings 2-5 have 3 choices, so we have  $4\cdot 3^4$ , but we have 3 choices for ring 6 as ring 1 and ring 5 are the same, so we get  $4\cdot 3^5\cdot\frac{7}{27}$ . Now, for ring 1 and ring 5 being different colors we still have  $4\cdot 3^4$ , but this time it will be times  $\frac{20}{27}$  and we need to multiply by 2 as well since there are now 2 choices for ring 6. We now have  $4\cdot 3^4\cdot 2\cdot\frac{20}{27}$ , adding these two values up we get  $\boxed{732}$ .</details>

