A slightly faster trig solution.
[asy]
size(150);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.8)+fontsize(11pt));
pair A = dir(90 + 3 * 20), B = dir(90 + 20), C = dir(90 - 20), D = dir(90 - 3 * 20);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);
draw(unitcircle);
draw(C--origin--D,linetype("4 4"));
draw(B--foot(B,A,D),linetype("4 4"));
draw(rightanglemark(A,foot(B,A,D),B,2));
label(" $A$ ",A,dir(origin--A));
label(" $B$ ",B,dir(origin--B));
label(" $C$ ",C,dir(origin--C));
label(" $D$ ",D,dir(origin--D));
label(" $O$ ",origin,S);
label(" $P$ ",foot(B,A,D),S);
[/asy]

The key is to note that  $\angle BAP=\angle COD$ .  This is because both angles equal the measure of arc  $\widehat{CD}$ .  (This is only true because  $AB=BC=CD$  is given.)

Now by Law of Cosines on  $\triangle COD$  (or, if you really want no trig, drop an altitude from  $C$  to  $OD$  and find the relevant measurements through Pythag), compute  $\cos\angle COD=\cos\angle BAD=\tfrac34$ .  This means that  $AP=\tfrac34\cdot 200=150$ , so the answer is  $200+2\cdot 150=\boxed{500\textbf{ (E)}}$ .

