Because no one else has the heart, here is the coord bash + shoelace solution.

Let the bottom left corner be the origin and the rest of the points be where you think they are (actually I do not trust you so  $A=(0,0),B=(0,5),C=(8,5),D=(8,0)$  and  $E=(0,4),F=(1,5),G=(8,1),H=(7,0)$ ). The line through  $E=(0,4)$  and  $G=(8,1)$  is defined by the equation  $y=-\frac 38 x + 4$ . The line through  $F=(1,5)$  and  $H=(7,0)$  is defined by the equation  $y=-\frac 56x+\frac{35}6$ . To find their intersection point,  $X$ , we solve \begin{align*}
y&=-\frac 38 x + 4
y&=-\frac 56x+\frac{35}6
\end{align*} which we can do with Augmented Matrices as follows   $$ \begin{bmatrix} 
\frac 38 & 1 & 4
\frac 56 & 1 & \frac{35}{6}
\end{bmatrix}\Rightarrow\begin{bmatrix} 
\frac {9}{24} & 1 & \frac{24}{6}
\frac {20}{24} & 1 & \frac{35}{6}
\end{bmatrix}\Rightarrow \begin{bmatrix} 
\frac {9}{24} & 1 & \frac{24}{6}
\frac {11}{24} & 0 & \frac{11}{6}
\end{bmatrix}\Rightarrow\begin{bmatrix} 
\frac {9}{24} & 1 & \frac{24}{6}
1 & 0 & 4
\end{bmatrix} $$   so  $X=(x,y)=(4,\tfrac {15}{6})=(4,\tfrac{5}{2})$ .

Now we use the following which we state without proof:**Theorem:** Given the coordinates of its vertices, the Shoelace Formula allows us to find the area of a polygon in the coordinate plane. Specifically, given a polygon  $A_1A_2\dots A_n$  with vertices  $(x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\dots (x_n,y_n),$  the area of  $A_1A_2\dots A_n,$  denoted  $[A_1A_2\dots A_n],$  is  $$ \frac{1}{2}\lvert (\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} x_iy_{i+1})+x_ny_1-(\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} x_{i+1}y_i)-x_1y_n\rvert. $$  This can be rewritten as  $$ \frac{1}{2}\lvert (x_1y_2+x_2y_3+\dots x_ny_1) - (x_2y_1+x_3y_2+\dots x_1y_n)\rvert. $$    $\square$ We can express the Shoelace Formula in determinant form for a triangle as   $$ \frac 12\begin{vmatrix}
x_1&y_1&1
x_2&y_2&1
x_3&y_3&1
\end{vmatrix} $$   where  $(x_i,y_i)$  for  $i\in \{1,2,3\}$  are the three vertices of the triangle.

Using Shoelace on triangles  $EBX,FBX,GDX,$  and  $HDX$ , we find that \begin{align*}
[EBX]=\frac 12 \begin{vmatrix}
0&4&1
0&5&1
4&\tfrac 52&1
\end{vmatrix}=2
[FBX]=\frac 12\begin{vmatrix}
0&5&1
1&5&1
4&\tfrac 52&1
\end{vmatrix}=\frac 54
[GDX]=\frac 12\begin{vmatrix}
8&1&1
8&0&1
4&\tfrac 52&1
\end{vmatrix}=2
[HDX]=\frac 12\begin{vmatrix}
8&0&1
7&0&1
4&\tfrac 52&1
\end{vmatrix}=\frac 54
\end{align*} because the areas are signed. Hence, the area of the shaded region is   $$ \sum_{n=1}^1\frac{n!}2(2^{n!}+\tfrac 54^{n!} + 2^{n!} + \tfrac 54^{n!})=\frac{13}{2} $$   so circle answer choice  $D$ . We will bubble it in later because of procrastination.
