Consider a huge  $n$  ,such that  $n > K^{100000}$  . We claim that the number  $x=(n!)^{d-1}$  works . 

To see this , we prove that  $x$  can be written as a  $d$  digit palindrome for each  $ \frac {n!}{i} -1$  with  $1 \leq i < n$  .

Indeed we claim that   $$  x = \sum_{i=0}^{d-1} k^{d-1} \binom {d-1}{i} \left( \frac {n!}{k} -1 \right)^{i} $$  When we write this in base  $ \frac {n!}{k} -1$  ,it is clear that it is a palindrome . Also the fact that  $n$  is huge, ensures that all the  $k^{d-1} \binom {d-1}{i} < \frac {n!}{k} -1$ .

Now we prove our claim . This is just a matter of carrying out a summation. 

We have :  $$  \sum_{i=0}^{d-1} k^{d-1} \binom {d-1}{i} \left( \frac {n!}{k} -1 \right)^{i} = k^{d-1} \sum_{i=0}^{d-1} \binom {d-1}{i} \left( \frac {n!}{k} -1 \right)^{i}
=  k^{d-1}  \left( \frac {n!}{k} -1 +1 \right)^{d-1}
=  k^{d-1}  \left( \frac {n!}{k}\right)^{d-1}
={(n!)}^{d-1} $$  We're done  $\blacksquare$

