<details><summary>Solution</summary>Without loss of generality, let  $(ABC)$  be the unit circle.
Let  $x$  be the center of  $(PDL)$ . Defining  $y$  and  $z$  similarly, it suffices to show that  $x,y,z$  are collinear.

Obviously,  $x$  lies on chord  $bc$  of the unit circle (the angle bisector of  $pd$ ). Thus,  $\overline x=\frac{b+c-x}{bc}$ .

Now, we use the fact that  $XP=XL$  to solve for  $x$ .
\begin{align*}
(x-p)\overline{(x-p)}&=(x-l)\overline{(x-l)}
(x-p)(\overline x-\overline p)&=\left(x-\frac{p-a}{1-a\overline p}\right)\left(\overline x-\frac{1-a\overline p}{p-a}\right)
\left(\frac{1-a\overline p}{p-a}-\overline p\right)x+\left(\frac{p-a}{1-a\overline p}-p\right)\overline x&=1-p\overline p
\left(\frac{1-p\overline p}{p-a}\right)x+\left(\frac{ap\overline p-a}{1-a\overline p}\right)\overline x&=1-p\overline p
\left(\frac1{p-a}\right)x+\left(\frac a{a\overline p-1}\right)\left(\frac{b+c-x}{bc}\right)&=1
bc(a\overline p-1)x+(p-a)(a)(b+c-x)&=(p-a)(a\overline p-1)bc
x&=\frac{abcp\overline p-a^2bc\overline p-bcp-cap-abp+abc+a^2b+a^2c}{abc\overline p-bc-ap+a^2}
&=\frac{(a-p)(bc+ca+ab-abc\overline p)}{abc\overline p-bc-ap+a^2}
\overline x&=\frac{(a\overline p-1)(a+b+c-p)}{abc\overline p-bc-ap+a^2}
\end{align*}

Now, we compute the determinant  $\left|\begin{matrix}x&\overline x&1y&\overline y&1z&\overline z&1\end{matrix}\right|$ . First, define the alternating symmetric sum  $\sum\limits_{\text{sym}'}f((a,b,c))=\sum\limits_{\pi\in S_3}\text{sgn}(\pi)f(\pi(a,b,c))$ .

Then the determinant is
\begin{align*}
\left|\begin{matrix}x&\overline x&1y&\overline y&1z&\overline z&1\end{matrix}\right|&=\sum\limits_{\text{sym}'}x\overline y
&=\frac{\sum\limits_{\text{sym}'}(a-p)(bc+ca+ab-abc\overline p)(b\overline p-1)(a+b+c-p)(abc\overline p-ab-cp+c^2)}{\prod\limits_{\text{cyc}}abc\overline p-bc-ap+a^2}
&=\frac{(bc+ca+ab-abc\overline p)(a+b+c-p)\sum\limits_{\text{sym}'}(a-p)(b\overline p-1)(abc\overline p-ab-cp+c^2)}{\prod\limits_{\text{cyc}}abc\overline p-bc-ap+a^2}
&=\frac{(bc+ca+ab-abc\overline p)(a+b+c-p)\sum\limits_{\text{sym}'}-ab^2cp\overline p^2+bcp^2\overline p+a^2b^2c\overline p^2-cp^2+(ab^2-bc^2)p\overline p+(abc^2-a^2bc-a^2b^2)\overline p+(-ab+ac+c^2)p+a^2b-ac^2}{\prod\limits_{\text{cyc}}abc\overline p-bc-ap+a^2}
\end{align*}
And it can be easily confirmed that every term here disappears under the alternating symmetric sum, completing the proof.</details>

