I claim that the answer is  $\boxed{2n-4}.$  A construction is the following configuration: 

[asy]
size(160);
int n = 8;
real s = 1;

for (int i = 0; i <= n; ++i) {
  draw((0,i*s)--(n*s,i*s));
  draw((i*s,0)--(i*s,n*s));
}

for (int i = 0; i < n-1; ++i) {
  fill((i*s,(n-1)*s)--((i+1)*s,(n-1)*s)--((i+1)*s,n*s)--(i*s,n*s)--cycle, black);
}

for (int j = 0; j < n-1; ++j) {
  fill(((n-1)*s,j*s)--(n*s,j*s)--(n*s,(j+1)*s)--((n-1)*s,(j+1)*s)--cycle, black);
}
[/asy]
i.e. when all squares in the top row are colored black except for the top-right corner square and all squares in the right-most column are colored black except for the top-right corner square. 

Toggling the top row and then toggling each of the left-most  $n-1$  columns gives us a completely black grid, so this construction works. Hence, it remains to prove that the bound holds. 

We can rephrase the problem in the following way: If we begin with an  $n \times n$  board that is entirely black, what is the least number of black squares that there can be on the board after some number of moves such that two of them are opposite corner squares of the  $n \times n$  board. 

Now, observe that toggling any row or column twice has no effect, so without loss of generality, let us toggle each of  $x$  rows exactly once and toggle each of  $y$  columns exactly once, beginning at the all-black configuration. Then, there will be  $xy$  squares that are toggled twice and  $(n-x)(n-y)$  squares that are toggled zero times, for a total of   $$ xy+(n-x)(n-y) $$   squares that will be toggled an even number of times. These are the squares that will be black at the end of the process, so we wish to minimize this expression. 

Firstly, since we want at least two squares to be black at the end of the process (two opposite corner squares), we want to have  $x, y < n.$  Also, if we toggle zero rows or zero columns, then we will be left with at least  $2n$  black squares in order to have two corner squares of the  $n \times n$  grid, so we can also assume that  $x,y>0.$  Then, using the AM-GM inequality and the obvious fact that the function   $$ f(z)=z(n-z) $$   is minimized over  $z>0$  when  $z$  is as close as possible to either  $0$  or  $n,$  we get
\begin{align*}
    xy+(n-x)(n-y) &\ge 2\sqrt{x(n-x)y(n-y)} 
    &\ge 2\sqrt{1 \cdot (n-1) \cdot 1 \cdot (n-1)} 
    &= 2(n-1) 
    &= 2n-2.
\end{align*}
Equality can obviously hold for both the AM-GM Inequality and the inequality:   $$ f(z) \ge n-1 \ \forall \  z \in \mathbb{Z}_{1 \le z \le n-1} $$   (i.e. for  $(x,y)=(1,n-1)$  or  $(n-1, 1)$ ), so we are done.  $\square$

