for (int i = 0; i <= 7; ++i) {
  draw((0,i)--(7,i), black);
  draw((i,0)--(i,7), black);
}
for (int i = 1; i <= 6; ++i) {
  filldraw((i,0)--(i,1)--(i+1,1)--(i+1,0)--cycle, palered);
  filldraw((0,i)--(1,i)--(1,i+1)--(0,i+1)--cycle, palered);
}
[/asy]
This will clearly work, and has  $2n-4$  additional cells colored.

We now provide a proof of bound. Let  $R_i$  denote the number of times the row  $i$  is inverted, and similarly for  $C_i$ . In addition, let the top-left and bottom-right corners be shaded, and  $\{R\}$  as well as  $\{C\}$  be taken modulo  $2$ . Notice for a valid configuration, if the cell  $(i, j)$  is shaded, then \[C_i+R_j \equiv 1 \pmod{2}\] and if the cell is not shaded, then \[C_i+R_j \equiv 0 \pmod{2}.\] Now, notice that when considered pairwise, the columns have to be inversions of each other or the same, and similarly for the rows, since after selecting a  $R_1$ ,  $\{C\}$  is determined by the cells of the first row. Thus, we can now case on the ``shadedness'' of the other two corners.


- If all of the corners are shaded, then the minimum number of additional shaded cells is clearly going to be  $2n-2$ , since each row/column must have at least two shaded cells.
- If three of the corners are shaded, then there is no way to get rid of the corners, since an inversion will get rid of an even number of the corners, and we can never reach  $0$ .
- If two of the corners are shaded (just the top-left, and bottom-right), then the first row and last row must be inversions of each other, so we have an extra  $n-2$ , and similarly, the first column and last column must be inversions of each other, so we have another  $n-2$ , giving a minimum of  $2n-4$ .

Thus, we have shown that the minimum across all configurations is  $2n-4$ , so we are done.  $\square$

