Construct a bipartite graph with all  $x$ -coordinates as vertices in one set, and  $y$ -coordinates in the other. Connect  $i$  and  $j$  if square  $(i,j)$  is colored blue. 
Now, since each blue square must be related to at least two other blue squares, this means that every edge must be sharing a vertex with at least two other edges. This forces each connected component to have at least  $4$  vertices, meaning that we can have at most  $2m$  connected components. Now using the formula 
\[v-(\text{\# of connect components})=e,\]
we get that 
\[e\ge v-2m=8m-2m,\]
or  $6m$ . Therefore we must have at least  $6m$  edges, i.e.  $6m$  blue squares, as desired. This completes our proof.

