WLOG, assume that  $AB<AC.$  Since  $B'$  is the reflection of  $B$  over  $AC,$  we have that  $AB=AB'$  and  $\angle BAB'=2\angle BAC.$  Similarly,  $AC=AC'$  and  $\angle CAC'=2\angle BAC.$  Thus, there is a spiral similarity at  $A$  taking  $C'C$  to  $BB'.$  Therefore,  $A$  is also the center of spiral similarity taking  $C'B$  to  $CB',$  so  $C'B=CB'.$  Furthermore, we have that  $DB=DC.$  Also,
\begin{align*} \angle B'CD &=\angle B'CA+\angle ACB+\angle BCD  &=\angle AC'B+\angle BAC+\angle ACB  &=180^\circ-\angle ABC'+180^\circ-\angle BAC-\angle DBC &=360^\circ-\angle C'BD &=\angle C'BD,\end{align*}so by SAS similarity  $C'BD$  and  $DCB'$  are similar. It follows that  $\angle C'DB=\angle B'DC.$  Thus, \begin{align*}\angle C'DB'&=\angle BDC+\angle C'DB-\angle B'DC &=\angle BDC &=180^\circ-2\angle BAC.\end{align*}
Let  $P,Q$  and  $R$  be the feet of the altitudes from  $A,B$  and  $C$  to  $BC, AC$  and  $AB,$  respectively. Let  $X$  and  $Y$  be the reflections of  $B$  and  $C$  over  $P,$  and let  $D'$  be the intersection of  $B'X$  and  $C'Y.$  By a homothety at  $B,$  we have that  $PQ||XB'$  and from a homothety at  $C$  we have that  $PR||YC'.$  Thus, \[\angle B'D'C'=\angle PQR=2\angle BAC,\]so  $D'$  lies on  $(B'C'D).$  Moreover, \[\angle YXD'=\angle B'XC=\angle QPC=\angle BAC\]and similarly  $\angle XYD'=\angle BAC.$  Therefore by ASA congruency  $YD'X\cong BDC.$  But since  $XY$  is the reflection of  $BC$  over  $AP,$  this means that  $D'$  is the reflection of  $D$  over  $AP,$  so  $O$  lies on  $AP$  as desired.

