<details><summary>Part (b) solution</summary>Let  $I$  be the incenter. Complex bash with unit circle  $(DEF)$ . Without loss of generality,  $ef=1$ . We have  $a=\frac{2f}{f^2-1}$ .

Since  $AI$  is the real line, we have  $x=\frac{d+f}{df+1}$  and  $y=\frac{df+1}{d+f}$ .

Claim: The equation of  $(AST)$  is
\[\left|\begin{matrix}1&-a&-z&az1&-\overline z&-a&a\overline z2&-x-y&-x-y&20&1&d^2&-2d\end{matrix}\right|=0.\]

Proof: For any  $z$ , each circle through  $A$  and  $Z$  is given by setting a specific linear combination of  $(w-z)(\overline w-a)$  and  $(w-a)(\overline w-\overline z)$  to  $0$ . The circle with diameter  $X$  and  $Y$  is given by setting  $(w-x)(\overline w-y)+(w-y)(\overline w-x)$  to  $0$ . The radical axis  $ST$  with equation  $2+d^2\overline w-2d=0$  can be derived as a linear combination of the two circles' equations. Thus,  $z$  lies on  $(AST)$  iff some linear combination of  $(w-z)(\overline w-a)$ ,  $(w-a)(\overline w-\overline z)$ ,  $(w-x)(\overline w-y)+(w-y)(\overline w-x)$ , and  $2w+d^2\overline w-2d$  vanishes. By expanding and taking the coefficients of  $w\overline w$ ,  $w$ ,  $\overline w$ , and  $1$ , this condition is equivalent to the given determinant vanishing.

First, flip the signs of the middle two columns and last row so that sign issues are less nightmarish:
\[\left|\begin{matrix}1&a&z&az1&\overline z&a&a\overline z2&x+y&x+y&20&1&d^2&2d\end{matrix}\right|=0.\]

We now substitute  $\overline z=\frac1z$ , set the determinant to  $0$ , and check that the discriminant of the resulting polynomial in  $z$  vanishes.

The coefficient of  $z$  in the determinant is
\begin{align*}
c_2&=a((x+y)(1-d^2)-2a)+(x+y)2d-2
&=\frac{2f}{f^2+1}\left(\left(\frac{d^2f^2+d^2+4df+f^2+1}{(df+1)(d+f)}\right)(1-d^2)-\frac{4f}{f^2+1}\right)+\frac{2d(d^2f^2+d^2+4df+f^2+1)}{(df+1)(d+f)}-2
&=\frac{2f(d^2f^2+d^2+4df+f^2+1)(1-d^2)(f^2+1)-8f^2(df+1)(d+f)+2d(d^2f^2+d^2+4df+f^2+1)(f^2+1)^2-2(f^2+1)^2(df+1)(d+f)}{(f^2+1)^2(df+1)(d+f)}
&=\frac{2(-d^4f^5-2d^4f^3-d^4f+d^3f^6-d^3f^4-d^3f^2+d^3+3d^2f^5+2d^2f^3+3d^2f-4f^3)}{(f^2+1)^2(df+1)(d+f)}
&=\frac{2(f-d)(df-1)(df^2+d+2f)^2}{(f^2+1)^2(df+1)(d+f)}
\end{align*}

The coefficient of  $\frac1z$  in the determinant is
\begin{align*}
c_0&=(x+y)2d-2d^2+a(x+y)(d^2-1)-2a^2d^2
&=\frac{2d(d^2f^2+d^2+4df+f^2+1)}{(df+1)(d+f)}-2d^2+\frac{2f}{f^2+1}\left(\frac{d^2f^2+d^2+4df+f^2+1}{(df+1)(d+f)}\right)(d^2-1)-\frac{8d^2f^2}{(f^2+1)^2}
&=\frac{2d(d^2f^2+d^2+4df+f^2+1)(f^2+1)^2-2d^2(f^2+1)^2(df+1)(d+f)+2f(d^2f^2+d^2+4df+f^2+1)(d^2-1)(f^2+1)-8d^2f^2(df+1)(d+f)}{(f^2+1)^2(df+1)(d+f)}
&=\frac{2(-4d^4f^3+3d^2f^5+2d^2f^3+3d^2f+df^6-df^4-df^2+d-f^5-2f^3-f)}{(f^2+1)^2(df+1)(d+f)}
&=\frac{2(f-d)(df-1)(2df+f^2+1)^2}{(f^2+1)^2(df+1)(d+f)}
\end{align*}

The coefficient of  $1$  in the determinant is
\begin{align*}
c_1&=4a^2d+a(2-2d(x+y)+2d^2-2d(x+y)+2+2d^2)-4d
&=\frac{16df^2}{(f^2+1)^2}+\frac{2f}{f^2+1}\left(4-\left(\frac{4d(d^2f^2+d^2+4df+f^2+1)}{(df+1)(d+f)}\right)+4d^2\right)-4d
&=\frac{16df^2(df+1)(d+f)+8f(1+d^2)(f^2+1)(df+1)(d+f)-8df(d^2f^2+d^2+4df+f^2+1)(f^2+1)-4d(f^2+1)^2(df+1)(d+f)}{(f^2+1)^2(df+1)(d+f)}
